    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: A name made only of replaced or trailing characters gives a folder name ending in a space**\n\n`validateWorkspaceName` accepts a name like `?`, `...` or `-`. In `newWorkspaceFolderName`, `?` turns into `-`, and the trailing-strip regex then removes it, so `safe` ends up as `\"\"`. The function then returns `\"Kaipu Record — \"`, which ends in a space: the same trailing space the doc comment says Windows refuses. On macOS a second name like that (for example `*`) collides with the first folder, and `create` refuses it as `exists`. A possible fix: when `safe` is empty, fall back to a placeholder, or strip the trailing space from the whole result. Another option is to reject such names in validation.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0. Runs of unsafe characters now become one dash and only dots and spaces are stripped from the end, so `?` gives `Kaipu Record — -`. A name that leaves nothing (`...`) falls back to `workspace`. Tests in `workspace-name.test.ts` check the folder name never ends in a space. `?` and `*` still map to the same folder; `create` then refuses the second as \"exists\" and the dialog says so, which I think is the right outcome."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597058,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/registry.ts",
    "line": 643,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Edit rollback leaves a newly written identity.json when the folder had none before**\n\nWhen the registry write fails, the rollback only restores the folder's file if `previous.kind === \"ok\"`. Take a pending entry whose folder is reachable but has no `identity.json` (`previous` is missing). `edit` writes a fresh identity at line 581 with the entry's id and the new name. If `update` then throws, that file stays on disk. The next probe finds an identity with the matching id and adopts it, so the change that was refused shows up again. Calling `removeIdentity(entry.path)` in the non-ok case would undo it the way `create`'s `undo` does.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: the rollback also calls `removeIdentity` when the folder had no file. In practice the probe at the start of `edit` rewrites a missing identity first, so `previous` is normally `ok` by then; I added a test for that real path (the old file is back after a failed registry write) and kept the `absent` branch as the guard for the race."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597062,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/index.ts",
    "line": 264,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Non-string color is reported as `invalid-name`**\n\n`createWorkspace` returns `invalid-name` when either `name` or `color` is not a string. If the payload is malformed only in its color, it should report `invalid-color`, which `WorkspaceCreateRefusal` already has. Otherwise the renderer would flag the name field. Splitting the check into two would report the right field.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: a non-string name reports `invalid-name` and a non-string color reports `invalid-color`, checked separately."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597064,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/main/workspaces/registry.ts",
    "line": 624,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🐛 **Consider · bug: Edit does not re-check name uniqueness inside the locked write, unlike create**\n\n`create` re-runs `validateWorkspaceName` inside `update`, so two concurrent creates cannot take the same name (line 515). `edit` validates only once, at line 540, before several awaits (the probe, `readIdentity`, `writeIdentity`). So two concurrent edits, or an edit racing a create, can both pass the check and leave two workspaces with the same name. The same `NameTakenError` re-check inside the `update` callback would close this gap.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: `edit` now re-runs the name check inside the locked `update`, throws `NameTakenError` and reports `name-taken`, undoing the identity write. New test: two concurrent edits to \"Same\" and \"same\" leave exactly one winner and the loser's `identity.json` untouched."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597067,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/workspace-dialog.tsx",
    "line": 353,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟠🐛 **Fix · bug: Edit refusals show messages about creating a workspace**\n\n`refusalMessage` is used for both create and edit, but some of its branches only make sense for create. When an edit is refused with `not-writable` (main returns this when the identity write fails; see `registry-create-edit.test.ts:384`), the dialog shows `createNotWritable`: \"Kaipu couldn't write there, so nothing was created.\" (`packages/i18n/messages/en.json:2605`). The `default` branch has the same problem: an edit refused with `invalid-name` or `invalid-color` shows `createFailed`, \"Kaipu couldn't create the workspace.\" (`en.json:2606`). Fix: pass `editing !== null` into `refusalMessage` and add edit-specific keys (for example `editNotWritable` / `editFailed`) in en.json and es.json.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: `refusalMessage` knows whether it is editing. Edit gets `editNotWritable` (\"Kaipu couldn't write to this workspace's folder, so nothing changed.\") and `editFailed` (\"Kaipu couldn't save the changes.\"), in en and es. The dialog test now asserts the exact text."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "comment_id": 4438597070,
    "path": "apps/kaipu-record/src/renderer/src/features/workspaces/workspace-dialog.test.tsx",
    "line": 155,
    "finding": "👮 **Wachiman** · 🟡🧪 **Consider · tests: The stale-answer test would still pass without the `alive` guard**\n\nAfter `answerFirst(...)`, the test waits for one `await Promise.resolve()` and then checks that the \"Wo\" path is absent. If the `alive` guard in `workspace-dialog.tsx:83` were removed, the late `.then` would call `setLocation` outside `act`. React schedules that render through its scheduler, not inside the same microtask, so `queryByText(...Wo)` would still return null and the test would pass anyway. To make the test catch the bug, wait for any pending render before the assertion, for example `await act(async () => answerFirst(...))`, or assert inside a `waitFor` that runs after a macrotask, and keep checking that the \"Work\" path is still shown.\n\n<!-- wachi:finding -->",
    "replies": [
      {
        "author": "csdev19",
        "body": "Fixed in 886d67f0: the late answer now resolves inside `await act(async …)`, so React flushes the render. I checked it has teeth: with the `alive` guard removed the test fails; with it restored it passes."
      }
    ]
  },
